No huge pages, until the C library's mmap refuses them

PostgreSQL asks for huge pages by mapping its shared memory with
MAP_HUGETLB (huge_pages = try or on), and takes a failure of that mapping
for "no huge pages reserved": try then maps ordinary pages and reports
huge_pages_status off, and on stops the server. The C library's mmap does
not look at MAP_HUGETLB and maps ordinary pages, so the server would
report huge pages that it does not have, and huge_pages = on would not
fail. This builds PostgreSQL for a system without the flag, as it does for
one that has none: try uses ordinary pages and says so, and on fails with
"huge pages not supported on this platform". The patch goes when mmap
fails a MAP_HUGETLB request with ENOMEM, as Linux does when no huge page
is reserved (docs/deferred.md).

diff --git a/src/backend/port/sysv_shmem.c b/src/backend/port/sysv_shmem.c
--- a/src/backend/port/sysv_shmem.c
+++ b/src/backend/port/sysv_shmem.c
@@ -39,6 +39,17 @@
 #include "utils/guc_hooks.h"
 #include "utils/pidfile.h"
 
+#ifdef __radix__
+/*
+ * The C library's mmap() ignores MAP_HUGETLB: the pages are ordinary ones,
+ * and a request for huge pages would succeed and be reported as granted.
+ * Build as a system that has no such flag, so that huge_pages = try uses
+ * ordinary pages and huge_pages_status says off, and huge_pages = on fails
+ * with PostgreSQL's own message.
+ */
+#undef MAP_HUGETLB
+#endif
+
 
